<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ethiopian Orthodox</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ethiopian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Tewahedo Church is the ancient Christian church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, belonging to the Oriental Orthodox communion. It was founded after the conversion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">King Ezana</a> in the fourth century and became a pillar of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, closely linked to the Solomonic monarchy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church.jpg" alt="Ethiopian Orthodox Church" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Addis_abeba,_chiesa_della_trinit%C3%A0,_esterno_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The church is the largest of the Oriental Orthodox churches and the first indigenous Christian church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>. Tradition and early writers report apostolic missions to the lands now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> tells of a royal official baptised by Philip the Evangelist. The church was administratively part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Orthodox Church</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> from the first half of the 4th century until 1959, when Pope Cyril VI of Alexandria granted it autocephaly with its own patriarch.</p><p>Its name, Tewahedo, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a> word meaning united as one. It refers to the miaphysite belief, shared by the Oriental Orthodox churches, that in Christ divinity and humanity are united in one nature, against the two-natures doctrine decreed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Union with the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> continued after the Arab conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Coptic patriarchs sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. In 1439, in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a>, a religious discussion between Giyorgis and a French visitor led to an embassy from Ethiopia to the Vatican.</p><p>A period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> influence, which broke the connection with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, followed an initiative of Portugal, which was in conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the Sultanate of Adal over the trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. An Ethiopian envoy, the Armenian Mateus, was sent to Portugal in 1507, and an embassy under Dom Rodrigo de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1520. In the 19th century the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> appeared. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> reformed the church's finances and clergy appointments in 1942 and founded the Theological College of the Holy Trinity Church in 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Services are held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geez">Ge'ez</a>, the liturgical language, while sermons are usually given in the local language. The sanctuary of a church houses the tabot, an altar slab dedicated to the patron saint, which is carried in procession on holidays; it is the tabot, not the building, that is consecrated. Churches are either basilican, as at Axum, or round or oblong in a native style, and eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">rock-hewn churches</a> stand at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>. Church music is traced to Saint Yared.</p><p>The church keeps 250 fasting days, 180 of them obligatory for lay people, on which no meat or animal products are eaten. It holds that its church of Our Lady Mary of Zion at Axum houses the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a>, a claim that international scholars doubt. Its biblical canon is larger than most, including Jubilees and Enoch.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Ethiopian Orthodox Church</b>, which traces its institutional lineage directly to this conversion, outlasted the Aksumite dynasty by more than a millennium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Literacy rose and the <b>Ethiopian Orthodox Church</b> was disestablished in 1974 under a policy of state atheism opposed by most Ethiopians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian state rested on a double legitimacy, Solomonic royal descent and <b>Ethiopian Orthodox</b> Christian authority: the church gave it ideological coherence, and the emperor claimed sacred kingship from both sources.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>It purports to trace the origins of the Solomonic dynasty, the line of <b>Ethiopian Orthodox</b> Christian monarchs who ruled the country until 1974, to the biblical king Solomon and the Queen of Sheba through Menelik I. Modern scholarship considers its stories to have been largely created to legitimise…</blockquote><a href="/work/kebra-nagast">Kebra Nagast · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The churches have long drawn <b>Ethiopian Orthodox</b> pilgrims, and to some Christians Lalibela is one of Ethiopia's holiest cities.</blockquote><a href="/work/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">Rock-hewn churches of Lalibela · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1960s the tizita mode became one of the foundations of Ethiopian jazz, or Ethio-jazz, which joined it with elements of <b>Ethiopian Orthodox</b> Christian music and with Western instruments; Mulatu Astatke was its pioneer.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tizita">Tizita</a></li></ul></section>
